3. when an transaction is published it is broadcasted to...
a) the recipient
b) the bitcoin network
c) the P2P network
d) all of the above
correct: d)
explanation: The Bitcoin Network and the P2P (Peer-to-Peer) Network are the exact same thing.
Bitcoin doesn't have a central server; it is a global P2P network made up of thousands of computers (nodes) talking to each other. 
When you broadcast a transaction, you are broadcasting it to this P2P network. The Recipient (or their wallet software/node) is also 
part of that P2P network, so they will see the transaction pop up in their mempool as well.

4. How does the recipient verify the authenticity of the transaction?
a) with his own secret key
b) with the digital signature
c) with the public key of the sender
d) by solving the computational puzzle
correct: b) c)
explanation: The Digital Signature proves that the owner of the funds actually authorised this 
specific transaction and that the message hasn't been tampered with. The Sender's Public Key is used to mathematically verify that signature. 
The recipient (or the network) runs the public key, the signature, and the transaction data through an ECDSA verification algorithm. 
If it checks out, it proves the transaction genuinely came from the holder of the matching private key.

5.  The signature of the transaction is created using:
a) the secret key of the sender
b) the secret key of the recipient
c) a hash function
correct: a)
explanation: Only the person who owns the funds holds that secret (private) key, which is why it is the only thing capable of
generating a valid digital signature to authorise spending those funds. Anyone else trying to sign it would fail the cryptographic math test

